ntdll: Make Rosetta's x86 CPU look more like a real one.

Programs built for a Skylake/Zen class CPU, and the copy protection some games
ship with (Phantom Blade Zero), never get to their first window under Rosetta:
its CPU lacks features they check for, and a few of its answers differ from
hardware. Under Rosetta only:

- CPUID leaf 7 reports CLFLUSHOPT, CLWB and RDPID. Rosetta answers CPUID from
  tables in its runtime image (libRosettaRuntime), mapped read-only into every
  translated process; the bits are set in this process's private copy.
  Rosetta runs CLFLUSHOPT itself; CLWB and RDPID raise an invalid opcode and
  are emulated. WINEWRAP_ROSETTA_CPUID7=0 turns this off.
- An access through a non-canonical address is reported as Windows reports
  the general protection fault: an access violation reading address -1.
  Rosetta delivers a page fault at the address itself.
- Execution breakpoints set through a thread context (Dr0-Dr3, Dr7) raise
  their debug exception. Rosetta has no debug registers, so the breakpoint's
  page loses execute permission and a thread entering it is single-stepped
  to the breakpoint. The program's code is not modified.
- A thread reading its own debug registers gets back what it set, with Dr7's
  always-one bit 10, instead of the zeroes the server answers.

The approach (which CPU answers matter, and that Rosetta's CPUID tables can be
changed per process) follows the ntdll-rosetta-cpu-fidelity work credited to
Hrava in MathiasKowoll/MacGameFix; this is an independent implementation.

Applies on top of 0004, to the Wine tree in CodeWeavers' crossover-sources
(26.3.0).

